      "outputType": "markdown",
      "output": "# ONDA K2: direction sheet\n\n## 1. The film in three sentences\n\nONDA K2 is a product launch film made by code: 26.4 seconds at 1920x1080 and 25 frames a second, for an invented product, a cycloidal robot joint, under the line QUIET FORCE. Its object was modelled by Python in a 3D program, lit and path traced, animated as pure functions of its music's beat, finished by a 2D layer drawn by a page, and given generated music and effects synthesized from its own events. It was made in one day on one computer by one operator and helpers started from it, each owning one module; the product, its name and its design are invented for the film.\n\n## 2. The modules\n\n**The object** (`src/parts/`). It made 15 part groups, about 1.13 million triangles, every one a real shape at real size, modelled by Python in the 3D program. The parts are:\n- a true 23-lobe cycloid outline checked against its 24 pins;\n- a 24-slot stator with wound coils;\n- a 28-pole rotor;\n- an encoder disc with nine code tracks and a 1,024-line track;\n- a populated driver board;\n- a crossed-roller bearing with 60 rollers;\n- 20 screws.\n\nFor one shot it also made a six-axis arm on six K2 units (`src/arm.py`).\n\n**The look** (`src/look.py`, `src/lighting.py`). It made 18 materials and four camera-relative light rigs, path traced on the GPU, with AgX. There is one emitter: the ice-blue status ring, pulsing on every beat.\n\n**The animation** (`src/anim/<act>.py`). It made seven acts as pure functions of the music's beat. Every curve, duration and spring comes from the film's own motion language. The film's state is asked for at four sub-frames of every frame, so the motion blur is real.\n\n**The 2D layer** (`src/comp/`). It is a page that draws type, tags, the calliper, the break-up, the colour fields and the status light's halo. It draws them over and behind the rendered frames, frame by frame, in openly licensed faces. The documents do not name the faces.\n\n**The sound** (`src/sound/`). It made generated instrumental music chosen by its measured shape: 140 beats a minute, two drops, a hushed beat. It also made about 265 effects synthesized in code from the animation's own events. The documents do not say what generated the music.\n\n**The film.** The 660 finished frames and the mix, encoded.\n\n## 3. Time\n\nEverything is placed in beats of the music and turned into frames by:\n\n`frame = round((first_hit + beat * 60 / bpm) * fps)`\n\n- bpm: 140.0\n- first hit: 0.449 s\n- fps: 25\n\nThe seven acts, in order:\n1. open\n2. hero\n3. inside\n4. exploded\n5. break\n6. second\n7. lockup\n\nThe documents do not give the beat or frame at which each act begins or ends. The only beat they place is the wind-up, at beat 15.\n\n## 4. The gates the film went through\n\n1. Style frames.\n2. Two animatics at 540p.\n3. One motion review of every act (35 findings, each with what was done), and three motion reviews of the 2D layer.\n4. A decision record for every act.\n5. The final.\n\nThe documents do not say what the 35 findings were.\n\n## 5. What was measured\n\nMeasured by the session that made the film, on its final.\n\n| Measure | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Frames, length | 660 frames, 26.400 s, every frame different from the one before |\n| The 3D render at 1080p, final settings | 1,409 s for 660 frames (2.1 s a frame on average) |\n| The 2D capture and encode at 1080p | 1,186 s |\n| A whole 540p animatic (3D, 2D and encode) | about 6 minutes |\n| Loudness of the film's sound | -14.0 LUFS integrated, true peak -1.3 dBTP, the last 0.4 s fading |\n| Black | one stretch of 0.16 s (frames 49 to 52: the camera inside a hole, deliberate); no frozen stretch |\n| Flashes | at most three full-frame flashes in any second (the film's own limit), counted over all 660 frames |\n| The cycloid | 11.7 to 12.5 micrometres of running clearance between the disc and its pins at every angle tried |\n| Triangles | about 1.13 million in the joint; 362 thousand in the arm |\n| Wall-clock | about 5 h 45 min from the plan to the final, on a computer also running other work |\n| Spent on generated media | about $0.60 (seven music requests); nothing else was bought |\n| Helpers | 5 modellers, look, 2D, sound, 3 animators, 3 reviewers, 1 researcher |\n| The film as carried: video | the same 660 frames encoded again at about 4 Mbit/s in nine parts of three seconds |\n| The film as carried: sound | the mix at 192 kbit/s |\n| Playback in a copy of the canvas's preview | the page loaded its ten video files and the sound, showed 1920x1080, and played 26.42 s in 26.6 s of real time to its end, with no error |\n\n## 6. What a person judged, and what is known to be weak\n\n**Judged by a person**\n- The owner watched the final and said it may stand as final.\n- The music was approved by ear before the animation was set to it.\n\n**Known to be weak**\n- The motor's phase leads end about 2 mm short of the driver board (never in shot).\n- Threads are rings.\n- Only the top layer of each coil is real wire.\n- The arm's K2 units are a lighter copy of the joint.\n- In the wind-up (beat 15) the graphite wall mirrors the rim light and reads pale.\n- The motor shot's tags move fast and may not be readable.\n- More than half of each poster word is knock-out.\n- The film opens in the dark for almost four seconds.\n\n## 7. How this canvas holds the film\n\n- The film's frames are not rendered again here.\n- The finished film is carried as an init file and nine parts of three seconds (H.264). These are the ten video files the preview loaded.\n- Its sound is carried as one file (AAC).\n- The page feeds the parts to one video element so that they join without a gap.\n- Each module's sources are carried as an archive, with a few of its own check pictures.\n- The canvas reads and checks the film and its sources, and puts the film into a page that plays in this canvas's preview.\n\nThe documents do not give the archives' names or sizes, or the number of check pictures.",
