      "brief": "You keep the sound: its sources, the cue list, the mix and its two stems of ONDA K2. 4 files are attached to this task and are in your workspace's inputs (see them with workspace_list; each is stored as upload-<8 characters>-<its name>). Nothing is made again here: the film is finished, and what you hold is what it was made from. Change nothing in the files.\n\n1. Read. Unpack the archive with Python's tarfile into a folder. With ONE script take from the sources: from out/sound/cues.json how many cues there are in all and by kind; from the sources the names of the synthesized voices or cue kinds cues.py and synth.py define (names only); and for each MP3 its length in seconds and its bit rate (count its MPEG frames with Python; there is no ffprobe). Compute each attached file's SHA-256 in the same script. You cannot hear the files: say nothing about how they sound.\n2. Hand on. Copy each attached file to its own name (the part after upload-<8 characters>-) at the top of the workspace and publish it into its output with workspace_publish: onda-k2-mix.mp3 into onda_k2_mix_mp3, onda-k2-music.mp3 into onda_k2_music_mp3, onda-k2-sfx.mp3 into onda_k2_sfx_mp3, onda-k2-sound-src.tar.gz into onda_k2_sound_src_tar_gz. The receipt of each publish gives the file's SHA-256: it must equal the one you computed from the input; if one differs, copy again and publish again.\n3. Report in result (Markdown): the cues' counts, the voices by name, and each MP3's seconds and bit rate against the film's 26.4 s. Then a table of the files handed on: name, bytes, the first 12 characters of its SHA-256, and \"same bytes: yes\" once its receipt agreed.\n\nPlan your steps: one script for step 1 (never print a whole source file or a binary file; keep what each command prints under 60 lines), 4 publishes, then the result. Use only what you read and measured; where something is not in the files, say so."
