      "brief": "You keep the film's init file and its parts 1 to 3 of ONDA K2. 4 files are attached to this task and are in your workspace's inputs (see them with workspace_list; each is stored as upload-<8 characters>-<its name>). Nothing is made again here: the film is finished, and what you hold is what it was made from. Change nothing in the files.\n\n1. Read. With one Python script read each MP4 file's boxes (an MP4 is a sequence of boxes: four bytes of length, four of type): for the init file, the track's width and height and its timescale (tkhd, mdhd) and the sample entry's type (avc1); for each part, its moof's sequence number, the number of samples and their total duration (tfhd and trun), so its frames and its seconds; for a sound file (.m4a) its sample rate and its seconds. Compute each attached file's SHA-256 in the same script.\n2. Hand on. Copy each attached file to its own name (the part after upload-<8 characters>-) at the top of the workspace and publish it into its output with workspace_publish: onda-k2-init.mp4 into onda_k2_init_mp4, onda-k2-part-01.mp4 into onda_k2_part_01_mp4, onda-k2-part-02.mp4 into onda_k2_part_02_mp4, onda-k2-part-03.mp4 into onda_k2_part_03_mp4. The receipt of each publish gives the file's SHA-256: it must equal the one you computed from the input; if one differs, copy again and publish again.\n3. Report in result (Markdown): a table of the files: name, bytes, what it is (init, part number, sound), frames and seconds for each part, and the sum of the parts' frames and seconds. Then a table of the files handed on: name, bytes, the first 12 characters of its SHA-256, and \"same bytes: yes\" once its receipt agreed.\n\nPlan your steps: one script for step 1 (never print a whole source file or a binary file; keep what each command prints under 60 lines), 4 publishes, then the result. Use only what you read and measured; where something is not in the files, say so."
